# Force LF line endings on checkout for every platform, regardless of a
# contributor's local `core.autocrlf` setting.
#
# Why this exists: kali-tools/Dockerfile embeds shell scripts via BuildKit
# heredocs (COPY <<'EOF' /usr/local/bin/apt-install-retry.sh). Without this
# file, a Windows clone with the common `core.autocrlf=true` default silently
# rewrites the Dockerfile's LF endings to CRLF, which bakes a CRLF into the
# heredoc-embedded script content. The resulting file's shebang becomes
# `#!/bin/sh\r`, which the kernel can't resolve — the script fails at build
# time with "not found" (exit 127) even though the file exists and is
# executable. Same failure mode applies to every other shebang script the
# build relies on (retry-cmd, gh-latest-asset.py, scripts/setup.sh, ...).
# Pinning eol=lf here removes the dependency on any user's git config.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Anything with a shebang or executed as a script inside a container must be
# LF no matter what — explicit belt-and-suspenders on top of the wildcard rule.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.cfg text eol=lf
*.ini text eol=lf
*.env text eol=lf
*.env.example text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts stay CRLF — that's the convention Windows tooling
# expects, and PowerShell doesn't care either way.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never touch line endings, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
